{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789406255,"nonce":"2mrvwN5iHtKIQuvpCfgb5jlaB5c=","title":"Python: a counted loop over typed lists cannot vectorize or hoist its data pointers","message":"`for i in range(n): ys[i] = a * xs[i] + ys[i]` over `list[float]`\nlowers each element access to the Python index protocol: a negative\nindex fold, a bounds compare branching to a cold `zb_fatal`, the load\nor store, and the pending-exception check after it. The axpy body is\nten conditional branches and three cold calls, so `loop_vectorize`\n(single-block bodies only) and `auto_vectorize` skip it, and LICM\ncannot hoist the `load %xs` data pointers past the cold calls, so\nthey are reloaded on every iteration. FMA contraction does fire. The\nsame loop in ZynML is one block.\n\nFix: loop versioning at the frontend or in HIR. For a loop whose\nindex is the counter of `range(len(xs))`, `range(n)` or `range(a, b)`\nwith a unit step and whose subscripts are the counter (plus a\nconstant), test once before the loop that every list is long enough\nand that no statement in the body can change a list's length (no\nmethod call on it, no call that receives it, no store to a name it\naliases), then run a body with plain GEP loads and stores and no\npending check; fall back to today's loop otherwise. With the cold\ncalls gone the data-pointer loads hoist, the body is one block, and\nthe vectorizers see the ZynML shape. This also removes two compares\nand a check load per access on the scalar path, which is the residue\nnbody and the list kernels pay now (5caf6de).","files":null}]}